          "segment_id": "fixture-12",
          "text": "Headline\n4\nShutterstock © Sergii Molchenko\nQ2 2026\n“Demand for best-in-class space \nremains unwavering, as tightening \nGrade A availability continues to \ndrive rental growth across Central \nLondon.”\nCentral London\nO F F I C E M A R K E T U P D A T E\n| C E N T R A L L O N D O N \nOFFICE MARKET UPDATE\nShutterstock © S.Borisov\nOFFICE SUPPLY AND VACANCY RATE\nCentral London Offic"
        },
        {
          "locator": "pdf:page:4",
          "page": 4,
          "segment_id": "fixture-13",
          "text": "ral London office market. During the first half of 2026, Grade\nA accommodation accounted for 69.9% of all leasing activity,\nunderlining\noccupiers’\ncontinued\npreference\nfor\nmodern,\nsustainable and amenity-rich office space. As a result, Grade A\nvacancy has tightened further, while second-hand accommodation\ncontinues to dominate overall availability. Grade B space now\nrepresents 72.6% of total Central London supply, providing greater\nchoice for occupiers seeking more cost-effective soluti"
